==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{term|Eid}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{alternative capitalization of|Eid}}

===Etymology 2===
From English dialectal {{term|eid|lang=en}}, from {{etyl|non}} {{term|eið||an isthmus, neck of land|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{recons|aidan||isthmus, strait|lang=gem-pro}}, of uncertain origin, but probably from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{recons|h₁y-||to go|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|eið|lang=is}}, {{term|eiði|lang=is}}, {{etyl|fo|-}} {{term|eið|lang=fo}}, {{term|eiði||isthmus|lang=fo}}, {{etyl|no|-}} {{term|eid||isthmus|lang=no}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|ed|lang=sv}}. Compare {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|eo|eō|go, proceed|pos=verb|lang=la}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|ed}}, {{l|en|aith}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{UK|_|dialectal|Scotland}} An [[isthmus]] or narrow neck of land jutting out into the sea; a [[sandbank]] cast up by the sea across the head of an open [[bight]] or inlet and having a lagoon inside it.

===Anagrams===
* [[die#English|die]], [[EDI#English|EDI]], [[ide#English|ide]], [[IDE#English|IDE]], [[IED#English|IED]]

